{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CeLqqUMwgLsGLzWkSPBEA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QbM7fuvcpXH9kgMaSG3bhC","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\book-outline\\retrieval-architecture.md","content":"# Retrieval-Practice Architecture — KCNA\n\n**Book:** Kubernetes and Cloud Native Associate\n**Stage:** B3 — Retrieval-Practice Architecture\n**Date:** 2026-08-23\n**Inputs:** B1 domain analysis (concept map, dependency graph, 114 traps) · B2 chapter lineup (20 chapters, 17 content)\n\nRetrieval targets below cite B1 trap numbers in parentheses — `(t6)` = trap #6 — so downstream stages can trace a checkpoint question back to the confusion it is designed to detect.\n\n---\n\n## Target spacing\n\nPer skill Part 10:\n\n| Chapter | % questions from earlier chapters |\n|---|---|\n| 1–2 | 0% |\n| 3 | 10% |\n| 4 | 15% |\n| 5+ | 20–25% |\n\n**One adjustment for this book's shape.** The skill's table assumes Chapter 1 is a content chapter. Here it is orientation — exam mechanics, the 2025-11-24 blueprint change, and cloud-native framing planted for Ch 17 — and carries 0% exam weight. Chapter 3's 10% therefore draws from **Chapter 2**, not Chapter 1, and no retrieval item anywhere in the book tests Chapter 1 material. Exam mechanics are reference, not recall; testing them consumes a spacing slot and returns nothing.\n\n### What the percentage applies to\n\n| Pool | Counts toward retrieval budget? | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| ☆ **Taking Your Bearings** | **Yes** — this is the primary instrument | Skill Part 10 specifies checkpoints; Bearings is the checkpoint |\n| **Practice Questions** (end of chapter) | **Yes** — same percentage applied to the pool | Skill Part 15 already calls for cross-chapter questions here |\n| 🧭 **Soundings** | **No** — excluded by design | See below |\n| **Mock Exam** (Ch 20) | N/A — 100% retrieval by definition | Assessment, not spacing |\n\n**Soundings are excluded but do the work anyway.** Skill Part 11 requires Soundings questions to be answerable from prerequisites. In this book, \"prerequisites\" means the chapters named in B2's Prerequisites column. So every Soundings block is *already* a spaced retrieval event over earlier chapters — it simply isn't counted, because its purpose is reading-strategy calibration and a question written to serve the budget would distort that. **Drafting instruction:** when writing chapter N's Soundings, draw its prerequisite-answerable questions from N's B2 prerequisite chapters rather than from generic Kubernetes background. The spacing is then free.\n\n### Minimum item counts\n\nAgainst the 10-question Taking Your Bearings floor B2 establishes for content chapters. B4 sizes the Practice Questions pool; apply the same target percentage to it once sized.\n\n| Target % | Minimum retrieval items in Bearings |\n|---|---|\n| 10% | 1 |\n| 15% | 2 |\n| 20% | 2 |\n| 25% | 3 |\n\nChapters whose Bearings run above the floor (B2 anticipates 12 for the heaviest) scale proportionally.\n\n### The spacing floor\n\nFrom **Chapter 8 onward, at least one retrieval item must come from a chapter four or more back.** Without this rule the schedule degenerates into \"test the previous chapter,\" which is massed practice wearing spaced practice's clothes and produces none of the benefit. The per-chapter targets below all satisfy it; the constraint is stated so revision passes don't quietly break it.\n\n---\n\n## Retrieval schedule\n\n| Ch | Title | % | Items | Retrieval targets (earlier-chapter concepts) | Rationale |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **3** | The Ship's Company | 10 | 1 | Container vs VM and what an image actually contains (Ch 2, t30) · CRI as the Kubernetes↔runtime contract (Ch 2, t33) | Ch 3 names \"container runtime\" as a node component and hands it to the kubelet. The reader must already hold what a container is and what CRI standardizes, or the node-component inventory is four nouns with no referents. Functional retrieval: the callback is load-bearing for the new material, not decoration. |\n| **4** | Records of Intent | 15 | 2 | The control loop — desired vs current state, reconciliation (Ch 3) · kube-apiserver as the single front door (Ch 3) · etcd as backing store for all cluster data (Ch 3) · image immutability (Ch 2, t31) | Objects are *what controllers reconcile* and *what the apiserver persists*; retrieving the loop at the moment `spec`/`status` is introduced is the cheapest possible reinforcement. The etcd item is a deliberate plant for Ch 12's \"Secrets are unencrypted in etcd\" (t60). Image immutability rhymes with immutable ConfigMaps (t18) — same principle, two layers. |\n| **5** | The Smallest Vessel | 20 | 2 | `spec` vs `status` (Ch 4) · kubelet's job — ensure containers in the PodSpec are running and healthy (Ch 3) · ConfigMap and Secret consumption paths (Ch 4, t17) · labels on object metadata (Ch 4) | `spec` vs `status` retrieved here is the scaffold for Pod phase vs container state (t6) — the reader who holds the object-level distinction absorbs the Pod-level one as an instance rather than a new rule. The kubelet item sets up probe ownership and restart backoff. |\n| **6** | Fleets, Not Vessels | 22 | 2–3 | **Control loop / reconciliation (Ch 3)** · label selectors, `matchLabels`/`matchExpressions` (Ch 4) · Pod phases Succeeded and Failed (Ch 5) · `restartPolicy` is Pod-level (Ch 5, t8) · a Pod is scheduled once and never rescheduled (Ch 5, t9) | The heaviest functional retrieval in Part II. Deployment → ReplicaSet → Pod *is* the control loop instantiated; the reader who retrieves it here experiences Ch 6 as a worked example rather than a new taxonomy. t9 is the fact that makes \"controllers replace Pods, they do not move or repair them\" land, and it is the load-bearing correction for t21 (Deployment vs StatefulSet). |\n| **7** | Assigning the Berth | 20 | 2 | Resource requests and limits (Ch 5) · labels as the selection primitive (Ch 4) · `Pending` phase (Ch 5, t26) · node components and what the kubelet does after binding (Ch 3, t24) | Filtering *is* requests-and-limits arithmetic (PodFitsResources); `nodeSelector` and node affinity *are* label selectors pointed at Nodes. Both callbacks convert new syntax into applied prior knowledge. The Ch 3 item defuses t24 — the scheduler binds, the kubelet starts. |\n| **8** | Standing the Watch | 20 | 2 | Control-plane and node component inventory (Ch 3) · namespaces and namespaced-vs-cluster-scoped (Ch 4, t14) · etcd (Ch 3) · requests and limits (Ch 5) · unschedulable Pods stay `Pending` (Ch 7) | Version skew is not a list of numbers — it is a statement *about the components from Ch 3*, and it only becomes memorable when retrieved as such. ResourceQuota and LimitRange are namespace mechanics; drain produces the Pending state from Ch 7. First chapter where the four-back spacing floor binds (Ch 3, 4). |\n| **9** | Every Pod Has an Address | 22 | 2–3 | **Label selectors (Ch 4)** · readiness probes and what failure does (Ch 5, t10) · Pods are replaced with new IPs, never repaired (Ch 5/6, t9) · namespaces (Ch 4, t46) · kube-proxy is optional (Ch 3, t1) | A Service is a label selector with a stable IP in front of it; EndpointSlice membership is readiness-gated. Retrieving t10 here is the highest-value single callback in Part III — it converts \"readiness removes the Pod from endpoints\" from a memorized sentence into an observed mechanism. The Ch 5/6 item answers *why the Service abstraction exists at all*, which the chapter otherwise has to assert. |\n| **10** | Traffic from Beyond the Cluster | 20 | 2 | ClusterIP and the four Service types (Ch 9) · the controller pattern (Ch 3/6, t42) · label selectors and namespace selectors (Ch 4) · Pod-to-Pod reachability with no NAT (Ch 9, t35) | \"An Ingress with no Ingress controller has no effect\" (t42) is the controller pattern's *negative case*; teaching it as a fresh gotcha wastes the two chapters that established the pattern. The t35 retrieval is the precondition that makes NetworkPolicy's default-open posture (t48) surprising in the right way. |\n| **11** | Below the Waterline | 22 | 2–3 | **StatefulSet — Pods are not interchangeable (Ch 6, t21)** · namespaced vs cluster-scoped (Ch 4, t14) · the Node as a scope (Ch 3) · ConfigMap and Secret as volumes (Ch 4/5) · binding as a control loop (Ch 3) | Closes B2's single deliberate forward reference. The Ch 4 item is doing real work: PV is cluster-scoped, PVC is namespaced, and that one fact organizes t64 (which is supply, which is demand). The Node retrieval is the corrective for t65 — ReadWriteOnce is a *node* statement, and the reader who has to retrieve \"what is a node\" answers it correctly. |\n| **12** | Locks, Keys, and Watchstanders | 22 | 2–3 | **Namespaced vs cluster-scoped (Ch 4, t14 → t55)** · Secrets are base64, not encrypted (Ch 4, t16) · etcd stores everything (Ch 3, t60) · the three API access gates: authn → authz → admission (Ch 8) · NetworkPolicy is additive with no deny (Ch 10, t49) · ServiceAccount as Pod identity (Ch 5, planted) · registries and image provenance (Ch 2) | The Role/ClusterRole × RoleBinding/ClusterRoleBinding matrix (t55) is *nothing but* namespaced-vs-cluster-scoped applied twice; a reader who retrieves t14 cleanly derives the matrix instead of memorizing four rows. Pod Security Admission is an admission controller, so the Ch 8 gate sequence is a hard prerequisite. NetworkPolicy is **retrieved, not re-taught** — B1 sequencing implication #6. |\n| **13** | When the Cluster Won't Answer | **25** | 3 | Pod phase vs container state (Ch 5, t6) · `Running` ≠ working (Ch 5, t7) · liveness vs readiness failure signatures (Ch 5, t10) · requests and limits → OOMKilled (Ch 5) · unschedulable → `Pending` (Ch 7, t26) · image tags, digests, registries → ImagePullBackOff (Ch 2) · readiness → empty EndpointSlice (Ch 9) · unbound PVC → Pending Pod (Ch 11, t63) · node conditions and drain (Ch 8) · version skew as a failure cause (Ch 8, t27) | **Top of the band, and structurally so.** Troubleshooting is not a body of new facts; it is a retrieval discipline over Chapters 2–12. Every failure signature the chapter names resolves to a concept already taught, and the chapter's method — read the phase before you read the logs — is literally an instruction to retrieve. The Ch 8 version-skew item is placed deliberately: see the decay watch below. |\n| **14** | Packing for the Voyage | 20 | 2 | `apiVersion`/`kind`/`metadata`/`spec` (Ch 4) · Deployment and Service as canonical chart contents (Ch 6, Ch 9) · CRDs and the operator pattern (Ch 6) · ConfigMap vs configuration values (Ch 4) · immutability (Ch 2, t31) | Templates render *manifests*; a reader who cannot retrieve the four required fields cannot read a rendered template. The `crds/` directory is meaningless without Ch 6's CRD definition. The immutability retrieval frames chart-version-vs-release (t80) as the same principle a third time. |\n| **15** | The Chart Is the Truth | **25** | 3 | **Control loop — desired state, continuous reconciliation (Ch 3) ☀️** · rolling update mechanics, `maxSurge`/`maxUnavailable`, Recreate (Ch 6) · Argo CD as a controller, CRDs (Ch 6) · ConfigMap and Secret as twelve-factor III (Ch 4, t86) · Pod disposability and statelessness as factors VI/IX (Ch 5) · chart vs release (Ch 14, t80) | **Primary Zenith. The retrieval *is* the payoff.** The four OpenGitOps principles are the Ch 3 control loop restated against a Git repository; the checkpoint question that asks the reader to name the Kubernetes mechanism each principle mirrors is the moment the book's spine becomes visible. Skewed to 25% and weighted toward Ch 3 on purpose — this is the one place where retrieving material twelve chapters back is the point rather than a hedge against decay. |\n| **16** | Your Application, Their Cluster | **25** | 3 | Platform-side triage — phases, events, node health (Ch 13, reciprocal) · init containers and probes (Ch 5) · debugging Services and endpoints (Ch 9) · ConfigMap/Secret mount failure → CreateContainerConfigError (Ch 4) · StatefulSet identity and its PVs (Ch 6/11) · Service types vs `port-forward` (Ch 9) · the two-audience split (Ch 13, t71) | The application half of the Ch 13 ↔ Ch 16 arc. Its retrieval load is deliberately dominated by Ch 13 so the reciprocal cross-bearing is reinforced by testing, not just by prose: the reader is asked to decide *which chapter's method applies* before applying either. That decision is t87's mitigation. |\n| **17** | The Fleet and Its Charts | **25** | 3 | **CRI (Ch 2) + CNI (Ch 9) + CSI (Ch 11) + CRDs (Ch 6) as one pluggability story ☀️** · control plane vs data plane, Kubernetes layer (Ch 3, t101) · HPA and manual scaling (Ch 6, t103) · Service as what a mesh sits between (Ch 9) · declarative API and desired state (Ch 3/4) · immutability (Ch 2) · zero trust and mTLS vs NetworkPolicy (Ch 10/12) · release cadence vs supported versions (Ch 8, t29) | **Secondary Zenith.** The extension-points synthesis is a four-way retrieval by construction — the chapter cannot deliver it without calling all four interfaces back, and the checkpoint should make the reader produce the pattern rather than read it. t101 gets its own dedicated item: mesh data-plane/control-plane reuses Ch 3's exact vocabulary at a different layer, which B1 correctly identifies as a genuine collision rather than a coincidence. |\n| **18** | Reading the Instruments | **25** | 3 | metrics-server and the resource metrics pipeline (Ch 13, t72) · `kubectl logs` vs cluster log architecture (Ch 13) · microservices and mesh telemetry (Ch 17) · HPA's dependence on metrics (Ch 6/17) · readiness probe vs SLI — \"is it serving?\" at two layers (Ch 5) · Prometheus as CNCF's second project (Ch 17, t96) · scrape targets and Service discovery (Ch 9) | Last content chapter, so it carries the most accumulated decay and sits at the top of the band. The metrics-server item (t72) is the highest-value callback: it is the same \"the object exists but nothing happens without the component\" pattern the book has now shown five times. The readiness-vs-SLI pairing is the chapter's own small Zenith — both answer whether the service is doing what users expect, one at Pod scope and one at service scope. |\n| **19** | Bearings Before Landfall | **100** | — | All of Chapters 2–18 | Synthesis chapter. Outside the 20–25% rule by definition: every question is retrieval. Structure it around the cross-cutting themes below and the confusion pairs, not around a chapter-by-chapter walk. |\n| **20** | Full Mock Exam | **100** | — | All of Chapters 2–18, weighted 44 / 28 / 16 / 12 | Assessment. Domain weighting, not chapter weighting — the mock reproduces the blueprint, not the book's shape. |\n\n**Band summary:** Ch 3 at 10% · Ch 4 at 15% · Ch 5, 7, 8, 10, 14 at 20% · Ch 6, 9, 11, 12 at 22% · Ch 13, 15, 16, 17, 18 at 25%. Every content chapter falls inside the skill's specified band. The five chapters at the ceiling are the two Zeniths, the two troubleshooting chapters, and the final content chapter — each chosen because retrieval is the chapter's method, not a tax on it.\n\n---\n\n## Cross-cutting themes\n\nNine concepts recur across enough of the book that they deserve deliberate repeated retrieval rather than incidental mention. Each is listed with its introduction point, its retrieval waypoints, and the traps it defuses. Downstream stages should treat these as the book's memory spine — a chapter that touches one of these and *doesn't* retrieve it is leaving value on the table.\n\n### 1. Declarative desired state and the reconciliation loop\n\n**Introduced:** Ch 3 · **Retrieved:** Ch 4, 6, 11, 15, 17 · **Traps:** t4, t73, t74, t75\n\nThe book's spine and its most reused idea. It appears as: the controller pattern (Ch 3), what `spec` and `status` are for (Ch 4), what a Deployment actually does (Ch 6), how PVC binding works (Ch 11), what GitOps is (Ch 15), and what \"declarative API\" means as a cloud-native characteristic (Ch 17). A reader who can state it cold by Ch 15 gets the primary Zenith; one who cannot gets a fifth list to memorize. **This is the single highest-priority retrieval thread in the book.**\n\n### 2. The label selector as universal glue\n\n**Introduced:** Ch 4 · **Retrieved:** Ch 6, 7, 9, 10, 12 · **Traps:** t13, t15, t22\n\nLabels are how ReplicaSets find their Pods, how `nodeSelector` and node affinity find nodes, how Services find endpoints, how NetworkPolicy scopes `podSelector` and `namespaceSelector`, and how Pod Security Admission is applied per namespace. Five subsystems, one primitive. Retrieval should emphasize that the *same* mechanism is at work each time — that is what makes it cheap to learn once and expensive to re-learn five times.\n\n### 3. Namespaced versus cluster-scoped\n\n**Introduced:** Ch 4 · **Retrieved:** Ch 8, 9, 11, 12 · **Traps:** t14, t46, t55, t59, t64\n\nThe most reused *structural* distinction in the exam and the root of five separate traps. It organizes: what ResourceQuota can bound (Ch 8), why a bare service name doesn't resolve cross-namespace (Ch 9), why PV is cluster-scoped and PVC is not (Ch 11), and the entire four-way RBAC matrix (Ch 12). Ch 12's treatment should *derive* the matrix from this distinction in front of the reader rather than presenting it as a table to memorize.\n\n### 4. \"Additive only — there is no deny rule\"\n\n**Introduced:** Ch 10 (NetworkPolicy) · **Retrieved:** Ch 12 (RBAC) · **Traps:** t49, t53\n\nTwo subsystems, one shape, and candidates reliably get one right and the other wrong. Pair them deliberately: a Ch 12 checkpoint question that asks the reader to name *the other* Kubernetes subsystem with the same property makes the pattern explicit and inoculates both. Ch 19 should restate the pair.\n\n### 5. \"The object exists, but nothing happens without the component\"\n\n**Introduced:** Ch 10 · **Retrieved:** Ch 10, 12, 13, 17, 18 · **Traps:** t42, t47, t72, t104\n\nA *pattern*, not a fact, and one of the most testable shapes in the whole curriculum. Its instances: an Ingress with no Ingress controller does nothing (t42); a NetworkPolicy on a CNI that doesn't implement it does nothing — a silent security failure (t47); `kubectl top` without metrics-server returns nothing (t72); VPA is an add-on and is not installed by default (t104). Also present in negative form: kube-proxy is optional (t1), cloud-controller-manager is absent on-prem (t2).\n\n**Recommendation:** name the pattern once, in Ch 10, and retrieve it by name at each subsequent instance. Four unrelated gotchas become one rule with four examples, and a reader who holds the rule handles instances the book never listed.\n\n### 6. Control plane versus data plane\n\n**Introduced:** Ch 3 (Kubernetes) · **Retrieved:** Ch 17 (service mesh) · **Traps:** t101\n\nA vocabulary collision, not an analogy. Kubernetes' control plane manages cluster state; a mesh's control plane configures proxies that are themselves the data plane. Same words, different layer, and B1 flags it as genuine. Ch 17 must retrieve the Kubernetes sense explicitly before introducing the mesh sense, and the checkpoint should require the reader to distinguish them in one question.\n\n### 7. Pods are replaced, never moved or repaired\n\n**Introduced:** Ch 5 · **Retrieved:** Ch 6, 7, 9, 11, 13 · **Traps:** t9, t21, t31\n\nThe consequence chain: a Pod is scheduled exactly once (Ch 7) → so a controller replaces it rather than relocating it (Ch 6) → so its IP is not stable and you need a Service (Ch 9) → so storage must outlive it (Ch 11) → so the logs you want may belong to a Pod that no longer exists, which is why `--previous` exists (Ch 13). Five chapters, one property. It also underwrites container immutability from Ch 2 (t31), which is the same idea one layer down.\n\n### 8. \"Not secret, not encrypted, not by default\"\n\n**Introduced:** Ch 4 · **Retrieved:** Ch 8, 12 · **Traps:** t16, t60, t61\n\nA Secret is base64-encoded, not encrypted (t16); it is stored unencrypted in etcd unless encryption at rest is explicitly enabled (t60); and anyone who can create a Pod in a namespace — including indirectly, via a Deployment — can read every Secret in it (t61). The three land in three different chapters and are one story. Ch 4 should plant it, Ch 8 should retrieve it when etcd backup comes up (a backup of unencrypted Secrets is unencrypted Secrets on a disk somewhere), and Ch 12 should complete it.\n\n### 9. The counting facts\n\n**Introduced:** various · **Retrieved:** Ch 13, 17, 18, 19 · **Traps:** t27, t28, t29, t89, t97\n\nPure-recall items with the fastest decay curve in the book and no conceptual scaffolding to slow it: three supported minor releases · kubelet up to three minors older, never newer · kubectl one minor either direction · four Service types · four required manifest fields · four OpenTelemetry signals including Baggage (t89 — the one candidates drop) · four OpenGitOps principles · four Cs of cloud native security · three maturity levels in order (t97) · four initial namespaces · four probe mechanisms.\n\nThese need spaced re-testing *more* than conceptual material does, precisely because there is nothing to reason from. **Recommendation:** reserve one retrieval slot in each of Ch 13, 17, and 18 for a counting fact from a distant chapter, and give Ch 19 an explicit consolidated pass. The Lodestar (Stage B6) should carry the full set in one table.\n\n---\n\n## Decay watch\n\nReverse index of the schedule above: for each chapter, where its material is retrieved later. Chapters with thin coverage are at risk of being taught once and never revisited before Ch 19 — a two-hundred-page gap.\n\n| Source chapter | Retrieved in | Status |\n|---|---|---|\n| Ch 2 Containerization | 3, 5, 12, 13, 14, 17 | Healthy |\n| Ch 3 Cluster and controllers | 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 11, 12, 15, 17 | Strongest thread in the book |\n| Ch 4 Objects, labels, namespaces | 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 16 | Healthy |\n| Ch 5 Pods and probes | 6, 7, 8, 9, 12, 13, 15, 16, 18 | Healthy |\n| Ch 6 Workload controllers | 7, 9, 11, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18 | Healthy |\n| **Ch 7 Scheduling** | 8, 13, 17 | **Thin** — see below |\n| **Ch 8 Administration** | 12, 13, 17 | **Thin** — see below |\n| Ch 9 Services and DNS | 10, 13, 16, 17, 18 | Healthy |\n| Ch 10 Ingress, Gateway, NetworkPolicy | 12, 16, 17 | Adequate |\n| Ch 11 Storage | 13, 16, 17 | Adequate |\n| Ch 12 Security | 17 | Thin, but acceptable — Ch 12 is itself terminal for its material and Ch 19 carries it |\n| Ch 13 Troubleshooting | 16, 18 | Adequate; the Ch 16 reciprocal is heavy |\n| **Ch 14 Packaging** | 15 | **Thin** — see below |\n| Ch 15 GitOps | 17 | Adequate |\n| Ch 16 Debugging | — | Terminal; Ch 19 only |\n| Ch 17 Architecture and ecosystem | 18 | Adequate |\n| Ch 18 Observability | — | Terminal; Ch 19/20 only |\n\n**Three deliberate interventions**, already reflected in the schedule:\n\n- **Ch 7 (Scheduling)** carries taints, tolerations, affinity, and topology spread — a substantial block that Part III barely touches. Its retrieval anchors are `Pending` in Ch 13 (unschedulable Pods) and node autoscaling in Ch 17 (Cluster Autoscaler and Karpenter both act on unschedulable Pods, which is the same fact from the other side). Ch 19 must give scheduling its own synthesis slot.\n- **Ch 8 (Administration)** carries version skew — the densest pure-recall block in the book, taught in one chapter and otherwise never seen again. The schedule places version skew deliberately in **Ch 13** (as a troubleshooting cause) and release cadence in **Ch 17** (where the three-supported-minors rule and the ~3-per-year cadence explain each other, t29). Without these two placements, version skew is taught at roughly the 40% mark and next tested on exam day.\n- **Ch 14 (Packaging)** is retrieved only by Ch 15. That is acceptable — Ch 15 is immediately adjacent and retrieves it heavily — but it means Helm's chart/values/templates/release vocabulary (t79, t80, t81) gets one reinforcement. Ch 19 should carry a Helm slot, and The Lodestar should carry the chart-versus-release distinction.\n\n---\n\n## What must not be retrieved\n\nFour exclusions, each with a reason a revision pass would otherwise override:\n\n1. **Chapter 1 orientation material.** Exam mechanics, the eligibility window, the blueprint-change history. Reference, not recall. It consumes spacing slots and tests nothing examinable.\n2. **Anything tagged `[inferred]` in B1, framed as frequency.** Fourteen of B1's 114 traps are inferred from material structure rather than attested by a source. They are legitimate retrieval targets — t33 (OCI vs CRI), t85 (twelve-factor clustering), t87 (D2.3 vs D3.2) all earn their place above — but the surrounding prose must say \"easy to confuse,\" never \"frequently tested.\" Ethical Guardrail #8, and B1 §Known traps is explicit about it.\n3. **The dated graduated-project roster.** B1 recommendation #5: the roster changes between writing and the reader's exam. Ch 17 states it once, dated, framed as a snapshot. Retrieving it in Ch 18 or Ch 19 would train a list that may be wrong by exam day. **Retrieve the maturity *levels* and their order (t97) instead — those are stable.** The same caution applies to the current TAG list (t111): retrieve that TAGs were restructured in 2025, not a roster to recite.\n4. **The commonly-reported 60 questions / 75% pass mark.** Unpublished by the Linux Foundation (B1 G34). It appears in Ch 1 and Ch 20 as *commonly reported*, with the non-publication stated. A checkpoint question with it as the keyed answer would assert as fact something the book has explicitly said is not established.\n\n---\n\n## Notes for downstream stages\n\n- **B4 (Length Budget)** should size each chapter's Practice Questions pool with the retrieval percentages above already applied — a chapter at 25% needs its own-material question count sized against 75% of the pool, not 100%.\n- **B5 (Outline Assembly)** should carry the per-chapter retrieval targets into each chapter's outline block so the drafting stage sees them without re-deriving them. The trap numbers are the join key back to B1.\n- **Chapter Stage 8 (question-quality audit)** can verify this mechanically: count keyed answers whose concept belongs to an earlier chapter, divide by the Bearings-plus-Practice pool, and check the result against this document's target for that chapter. The spacing floor (one item from four-or-more chapters back, Ch 8+) is checkable the same way.\n- **Ch 19's synthesis** should be built from the nine cross-cutting themes, not from a chapter-by-chapter review. The themes are the structure the book has been building toward; walking the chapters in order at the end would restate the table of contents.\n- **The Lodestar (Stage B6)** should carry: the counting facts in full (theme 9), the additive-no-deny pair (theme 4), the four instances of \"nothing happens without the component\" (theme 5), the RBAC matrix derivation (theme 3), and the control-plane/data-plane collision (theme 6). These are the highest-density, fastest-decaying items in the book.\n\n---\n\n*Stage B3 complete. 16 content chapters carry retrieval load (Ch 3–18); Ch 19–20 are 100% retrieval by construction. Targets range 10% → 25%, all inside the skill's specified band. Nine cross-cutting themes identified; three decay interventions placed; four exclusions specified.*\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1247,"cache_read_input_tokens":122200,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1247},"output_tokens":4,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"46592f22-bd5b-4945-b7f7-384f0a58414e","uuid":"9d60c3fc-3206-4aea-9113-82ef02b61305","timestamp":"2026-08-24T03:16:55.987Z","request_id":"req_011CeLqqT25WBKADemEYUojQ"}
